        <body>
            <div xml:id="d744">

                <pb facs="0847" n="761" sameAs="frus:frus1903/pg761"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Leishman to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1903-12-13"><hi rendition="#italic">December 13,
                        1903</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 653.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring further to my telegram of
                    November 15 last, I am unable to report any material change in the situation of
                    school and other unsettled qnestions.</p>
                <p>The grand vizier and the minister for foreign affairs spend very little time at
                    the Porte these days, and when I do succeed in seeing them I am politely put off
                    on the score that nothing can be done until after Ramazan.</p>
                <p>In the absence of any instruction from the Department I continue the patient but
                    persistent role I have been playing since January, at same time exerting every
                    effort to preserve the friendly relations which, I am happy to say, remain
                    unchanged, despite the rather complicated and disturbed conditions. So that I am
                    in position to pursue any line of policy that the Department may dictate.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John G. A.
                    Leishman.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
